{"t":1795123947,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"8c8eabfd-865e-4e7c-8840-2e7f9ceaf96b","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-11-19T18:46:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 191846\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\nIssued by National Weather Service Mount Holly NJ\n146 PM EST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nNo significant changes for the next few days. Higher POPs and\nslightly greater rainfall totals for the late weekend system.\n\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Dry and warm conditions expected to continue through Saturday.\n\n- 2) Forecast remains uncertain as a coastal low tracks up the \n  Eastern Seaboard Sunday into Monday.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry and warm conditions expected to continue \nthrough Saturday.\n\nSurface high pressure remains overhead through Friday. This will\ncontinue to bring very dry conditions and very light or calm \nwinds. Highs will be above average with temperatures in the 60s \nto even the low 70s through Friday. Clear skies and light winds \novernight yield favorable conditions for radiational cooling. \nOvernight lows will drop into the 30s tonight with lows Thursday\nand Friday night staying in the mid 30s to 40s. \n\nWhile remaining overhead, surface high pressure begins shifting\nnorthward on Saturday as a low pressure system develops off the\ncoast of Florida and tracks north. This results in increased\ncloud cover although dry conditions and calm winds persist.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Forecast remains uncertain as a coastal low tracks \nup the Eastern Seaboard Sunday into Monday. \n\nAn area of low pressure is forecast to develop off the Southeast \nU.S. coast late this week, then track northward along the coast this \nweekend. The low's initial forward speed is going to be slow as it \ngets trapped underneath a brief, weak upper ridge, then quickly \npicks up speed as it moves northeast with a strong upper trough \napproaching from the OH Valley. There remains considerable \ndifferences among the global guidance, both run to run and between \nmodels in each run. The ensemble guidance for both the European and \nGFS remain quite dispersive with low tracks well inland across the \nNC/VA Piedmont to well offshore of the Mid-Atlantic. \n\nThere continues to be too much uncertainty to pin down any specific\ndetails or impacts to our area. Rain chances Sunday into Sunday night\ncontinue to slowly increase, especially east of the Blue Ridge with\nthe highest certainty east of I-95. The exact track of the low is\ngoing to be critical to where the heaviest rain falls, though there\ncould be a sharp gradient on the western side of the precip shield\nfrom heavy to very light rain amounts. It is worth mentioning \nthe higher-end of the ensemble spread indicates 2-4\" as a worst-\ncase scenario, but many members also have little to no rain for \nmost of the area - this probably speak the best to how much uncertainty\nremains. The location of the heaviest QPF also varies greatly from\nalong US-15 to the Delmarva. Continue to monitor the forecast \nover the next few days as guidance hopefully starts to converge \non a solution for this weekend. \n\nA strong Canadian High builds across much of the central to eastern \nCONUS during the early part of next week. This is going to bring us \ndry, brisk, and much colder conditions. \n\nModels are indicating the potential for another system near Thanksgiving,\nbut even more spread exists with that system. More updates as we\nget closer.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /18Z TONIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY/... VFR conditions at \nthe terminals through Friday. \n\nLight winds out of the south to southwest remain across the\nterminals the rest of today before becoming variable overnight.\nOn Friday, winds shift to north or northwesterly early and remain\naround 5 knots. Skies will remain mostly clear. \n\nWe continue to monitor the developing coastal low off the coast\nof Florida. As this low pressure system drifts northward, \nprecipitation and sub-VFR conditions are possible by Sunday and\ncontinuing into Monday across the terminals. The greatest \nchance will be for the eastern terminals, but there is still a\nlot of uncertainty with potential storm track at this time.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nHigh pressure will remain over the area tonight and Friday before\nretreating to the northeast. Light winds continue to support favorable\nconditions across the marine waters. A low pressure system is forecast\nto develop off the coast of Florida and may bring precipitation and\nincreased winds to the waters on Sunday which may approach SCA criteria.\nSCAs likely by late Sunday night through Monday as the low pressure\nsystem rides up the east coast. Stronger winds will be possible,\nbut will depend on the exact track of the low. Should the low trend\ncloser to the coast, Gale-force winds can't be ruled out Monday.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...None.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...O'Hara/CJL \nAVIATION...O'Hara/CJL \nMARINE...O'Hara/CJL\n"},"forecast":{"t":1795123799,"days":{"2026-11-19":{"hi":67,"lo":40,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-11-20":{"hi":69,"lo":42,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-11-21":{"hi":65,"lo":49,"pop":11,"short":"Partly Sunny"},"2026-11-22":{"hi":63,"lo":53,"pop":59,"short":"Light Rain Likely"},"2026-11-23":{"hi":65,"lo":39,"pop":23,"short":"Slight Chance Light Rain then Partly Sunny"},"2026-11-24":{"hi":50,"lo":34,"pop":9,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-11-25":{"hi":50,"lo":42,"pop":62,"short":"Chance Rain And Snow"}}},"grid":{"t0":1795122000,"n":48,"ceil":[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null],"vis":[10.0,10